<% content_for :title, "Where State Lives" %>
<% content_for :breadcrumb do %>
  <%= poetry_breadcrumb do |crumb| %>
    <% crumb.with_item("Docs", href: introduction_path) %>
    <% crumb.with_item("Where State Lives") %>
  <% end %>
<% end %>

<div class="flex flex-col gap-8">
  <header class="flex flex-col gap-3">
    <h1 class="text-4xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Where State Lives</h1>
    <p class="max-w-2xl text-lg text-muted-foreground">
      Poetry has no <code>useState</code>. Every state a component shows
      lives in a place the server, <code>poetry:check</code>, your CSS and
      the back button can see, and how long it must last picks the place.
      Five tiers, each with the component that ships it.
    </p>
  </header>

  <section class="flex flex-col gap-4">
    <%= poetry_typeset(preset: "docs", class: "max-w-2xl") do %>
      <h2 id="durable">Durable: the record and the form</h2>
      <p>
        The record is the truth and a component renders it: a Checkbox is
        checked because the row says so, and it changes by submitting.
        A <code>name:</code> makes any component a form participant
        through a real native control: Calendar keeps a hidden input the
        controller holds to the ISO date, Checkbox a visually hidden native
        checkbox, RadioGroup one native radio per item. The native element
        is the serialization truth, so what a test reads is what the server
        receives, and the page after the submit renders from the record
        again.
      </p>
    <% end %>
  </section>

  <section class="flex flex-col gap-4">
    <%= poetry_typeset(preset: "docs", class: "max-w-2xl") do %>
      <h2 id="addressable">Addressable: the URL</h2>
      <p>
        What a visitor should be able to share, bookmark and come back to
        lives in the URL. <%= link_to "DataTable", data_table_guide_path %>'s
        query, sort, direction and page are query parameters:
        <code>State.from_params(params, sortable:)</code> reads them back
        with the sort held to a whitelist, and the table renders inside a
        <code>&lt;turbo-frame data-turbo-action="advance"&gt;</code>, so
        each change is a history entry and the back button walks them.
        Collapsible's rule for disclosure the URL controls: render
        <code>open:</code> from params, and the same markup serves with
        and without JavaScript.
      </p>
    <% end %>
  </section>

  <section class="flex flex-col gap-4">
    <%= poetry_typeset(preset: "docs", class: "max-w-2xl") do %>
      <h2 id="preference">Preference: a cookie, or localStorage before first paint</h2>
      <p>
        A choice that outlives the page and must be right at first paint.
        Sidebar's controller persists the <code>sidebar_state</code> cookie
        when the column collapses; pass
        <code>open: cookies[:sidebar_state] != "false"</code> and the first
        paint matches the last choice with no flash. The color scheme goes
        the other way: <code>poetry_color_scheme_script</code>, rendered in
        <code>&lt;head&gt;</code> before the stylesheets, reads
        <code>poetry-color-scheme</code> from localStorage, or the OS
        setting when nothing is stored, and sets <code>.dark</code> before
        anything paints, so the server never needs to know. The rule is
        which side has to act on the choice: a preference the server
        renders is a cookie; one only the browser applies is localStorage,
        read before paint.
      </p>
    <% end %>
    <%= poetry_code_block(code: state_guide_snippet, language: "erb", label: "An option per tier") %>
  </section>

  <section class="flex flex-col gap-4">
    <%= poetry_typeset(preset: "docs", class: "max-w-2xl") do %>
      <h2 id="template">Template state, on a ReActionView host</h2>
      <p>
        An application on ReActionView has a tier between the URL and the
        element: a template declares a state, the server renders its
        default, and the browser owns it from there, for as long as the
        page lasts. Its limit: a Poetry component is rendered by the
        server, so a state read inside a component's block does not follow
        the state, and a row built from components cannot be painted before
        the server answers. Read the state beside the component, keep what
        a row shows at once in plain markup, and send a change the server
        may refuse through <%= link_to "poetry_optimistic_form", optimistic_forms_path %>.
        The <%= link_to "Herb and ReActionView guide", herb_guide_path(anchor: "reactive-templates") %>
        walks each case.
      </p>
    <% end %>
  </section>

  <section class="flex flex-col gap-4">
    <%= poetry_typeset(preset: "docs", class: "max-w-2xl") do %>
      <h2 id="transient">Transient: attributes on the element</h2>
      <p>
        Open, closed, checked, pressed, expanded, active: one vocabulary of
        bare boolean attributes (<code>data-open</code>, never a
        <code>data-state="open"</code> pair), written by
        <code>setState(element, key)</code> from Poetry's state helper,
        which sets the attribute, removes its counterparts and announces
        the flip as a <code>poetry:state-change</code> event. The server
        renders the first one (<code>open:</code>, <code>checked:</code>),
        the controller flips it, your CSS styles off it,
        <code>poetry:check</code> validates it, a test reads it, and a
        controller of yours listens for the change. What a controller needs
        between two calls and nobody else needs to see is a Stimulus value,
        declared in the controllers manifest. Whichever layer wrote an
        attribute owns it: never toggle <code>data-open</code> by hand on a
        component whose controller does.
      </p>
    <% end %>
  </section>

  <section class="flex flex-col gap-4">
    <%= poetry_typeset(preset: "docs", class: "max-w-2xl") do %>
      <h2 id="usestate">Why there is no useState</h2>
      <p>
        Every tier above is a place the checker, the server and the back
        button can see. A value held in a controller's memory is visible to
        none of them: it cannot be rendered on the first paint, cannot be
        validated statically, cannot be shared by a link, and goes when the
        page does. So Poetry's controllers hold nothing that is not on the
        element. Herb ships a <code>useState</code> of its own, from
        <code>reactionview</code>, for controllers on a ReActionView host
        that read template state; Poetry's controllers do not call that one
        either, for the same reason, and yours may. The
        <%= link_to "Stimulus guide", stimulus_path(anchor: "herb") %> says
        how the two share a page.
      </p>
    <% end %>
  </section>

  <section class="flex flex-col gap-4">
    <%= poetry_typeset(preset: "docs", class: "max-w-2xl") do %>
      <h2 id="morph">What a morph does to each tier</h2>
      <p>
        A refresh under <code>turbo-refresh-method="morph"</code> keeps the
        nodes the new page also has and syncs their attributes and form
        values to what the server rendered. The durable, addressable and
        preference tiers come back exactly, because the server rendered
        them. Template state survives in the browser. Transient state
        survives through Poetry's morph guard, installed with the
        controllers: a state attribute the client changed since the page
        was rendered is client-owned until the next full render, and the
        morph leaves exactly that attribute alone, so the selected tab, the
        open dialog, the expanded collapsible and the half-typed value stay
        as they are, while everything the client never touched follows the
        server, a cookie it read or a record it saved included. A form
        control follows the browser's own rule, kept while its value
        differs from the one the server rendered. A dialog whose own form
        submits successfully closes before the refresh, and overlays still
        close before Turbo caches a restoration snapshot, as they do before
        any navigation. The guard works on the nodes a morph keeps: a
        component without a stable id is replaced, state and all, so
        <%= link_to "Stable IDs", stable_ids_path(anchor: "morph") %>
        covers what pairing a morph needs from you.
        <code>data-turbo-permanent</code> with an id remains the answer for
        a subtree a morph must not touch at all.
      </p>
    <% end %>
  </section>
</div>
